Second-order RLC model in Simulink, validated analytically, with Monte Carlo component tolerance analysis.





To verify the model rather than trusting it, I solved the same system analytically using Laplace transforms and compared results. 
The closed-form solution predicts a steady-state charge of 5 C and a current peak of 2.5 A at t = ln(2) seconds; the simulation reproduced both, 
confirming the structure and parameter values were correct. I then wrote a MATLAB script to sweep resistance across the underdamped, 
critically damped, and overdamped regimes and characterize how the damping ratio governs overshoot and settling behavior.
Finally, I ran a 500-trial Monte Carlo analysis with all three components drawn from a ±5% tolerance band, since real parts never match nominal values.
The result showed an asymmetry worth designing around: steady-state output stayed tightly clustered because it depends only on capacitance,
while settling time varied considerably more because it depends on all three parameters through the characteristic roots. 
For an application where transient timing matters, component selection is more critical than the steady-state figure suggests.




//Code down here //



L_nom = 1; R_nom = 3; C_nom = 0.5;
N = 500;
tol = 0.05;

settle = zeros(N,1);
final  = zeros(N,1);

figure; hold on;

for k = 1:N
    L = L_nom * (1 + tol*(2*rand-1));
    R = R_nom * (1 + tol*(2*rand-1));
    C = C_nom * (1 + tol*(2*rand-1));

    out = sim('practice1');
    q = out.simout.Data;
    t = out.simout.Time;

    if k <= 50
        plot(t, q, 'Color', [0.5 0.5 0.5 0.15]);
    end

    info = stepinfo(q, t);
    settle(k) = info.SettlingTime;
    final(k)  = q(end);
end

xlabel('Time (s)'); ylabel('Charge (C)');
title('Monte Carlo: 5% component tolerance');

figure;
histogram(settle, 30);
xlabel('Settling time (s)'); ylabel('Count');

fprintf('Settling time: mean %.3f s, std %.3f s\n', mean(settle), std(settle));
fprintf('Range: %.3f to %.3f s\n', min(settle), max(settle));
fprintf('Final charge: mean %.3f, std %.4f\n', mean(final), std(final));
